WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.560
In 2017, Amazon announced that its Prime streaming
video service would soon be home to a truly

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.010
epic project: A TV series based on J.R.R.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.800
Tolkien's massive Lord of the Rings legendarium.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:16.280
While Lord of the Rings itself has already
been adapted for live action, quite successfully

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.789
in the form of Peter Jackson's Oscar-winning
film trilogy, this series will be set many

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:22.160
years before those events.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.729
We now know that Amazon's Lord of the Rings
prequel series will take viewers to the Second

00:00:26.729 --> 00:00:32.390
Age, a period of time that ended nearly 3,000
years before Frodo Baggins was born and culminated

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:37.040
in the War of the Last Alliance, which saw
Isildur cut the One Ring from Sauron's hand

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:37.540
in battle.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.720
The Second Age itself is also vast, covering
a little more than 3,400 years of history

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:47.550
that included wars, the rising and falling
of kingdoms, and of course the creation of

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.520
the Rings of Power, including the One Ring
to Rule Them All.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.880
There's a vast canvas for Amazon to work with,
and that means a lot of different characters

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.520
from Tolkien lore could pop up.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:01.590
Here are our picks for who we'd most like
to see, with a particular emphasis on characters

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:04.020
who haven't already starred in a big-budget
movie.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:09.219
Elendil is a character who appears for only
a moment in the prologue to the Lord of the

00:01:09.219 --> 00:01:14.530
Rings films, as the High King of Gondor and
Arnor who helped forge the Last Alliance that

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.620
was able to defeat Sauron at the end of the
Second Age.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.800
That's enough to make him a legend, but the
story of how Elendil got to that point is

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.760
what makes him one of the most compelling
characters in the Second Age.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.860
Elendil lived in the legendary kingdom of
Numenor at a time when its last king was corrupted

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:31.760
by Sauron.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:36.700
Even as the king led Numenor and its people
astray, Elendil remained faithful, and when

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:40.900
the kingdom fell, his life and those of his
sons and followers were saved.

00:01:41.389 --> 00:01:46.380
He sailed to Middle-earth, founded the kingdoms
of Gondor and Arnor, and set up a legacy for

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.490
men on that continent that carried all the
way through to Aragorn at the time of The

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:51.480
Lord of the Rings.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:56.920
It would be great to see this noble, beloved
king at the height of his power, or even resisting

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.580
Sauron's initial allure.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:03.660
Celebrimbor is one of the most important figures
in the history of the Rings of Power themselves,

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:08.460
and while he didn't appear in Jackson's films,
his spirit is a major part of the Middle Earth:

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:12.980
Shadow of Mordor and Shadow of War games because
of his closeness to the Rings.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:18.300
An Elven smith in the Second Age, Celebrimbor
was a skilled craftsman who was there when

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.490
Sauron came upon the Elves in his Annatar
guise and began teaching them how to make

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:22.980
rings.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:28.200
Though he didn't exactly trust Annatar, he
did participate in the crafting of rings,

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.800
and personally made the three Elven Rings
of Power.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.540
"Three were given to the Elves, immortal,
wisest, and fairest of all beings."

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:41.040
When Sauron revealed his treachery, Celebrimbor
worked to hide the Three Rings from the Dark

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.580
Lord so he could never wield their power.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:48.630
He died under torture, after refusing to give
up the location of the Three to Sauron.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:53.220
If you're going to touch on the story of how
the Rings of Power came to be, Celebrimbor

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.000
has a major role to play there.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:03:00.300
If you're a fan of the Lord of the Rings books
and films, you're obviously already very familiar

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:01.100
with Sauron.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.220
He's the main antagonist of J.R.R.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:08.320
Tolkien's novel and Peter Jackson's film trilogy,
and proved so vital to the overall mythology

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.870
of Middle-earth that he was retroactively
inserted into The Hobbit as "The Necromancer"

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.000
Gandalf was investigating at the time.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:19.620
It might seem like overkill to put the Dark
Lord in yet another story from this world,

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.820
but there's a good reason to bring Sauron
back.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:26.880
Sauron is a character older than the physical
realm of Middle-earth itself, an ancient spirit

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:32.710
created as one of the Maiar alongside other
vastly powerful beings like Gandalf and Saruman.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:37.030
That means you can find some version of him
in almost any part of the Tolkien timeline,

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:39.880
but in the Second Age he was particularly
devious.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:45.630
To forge the Rings of Power in the first place,
Sauron took on a beautiful, more angelic form,

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:50.080
re-named himself Annatar, and called himself
the "Lord of Gifts" to trick the Elves into

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:51.600
making rings with him.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.470
Seeing that version of Sauron, beautiful while
harboring a darker purpose, could lend a lot

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.260
of intrigue to the new series.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.380
Círdan is one of the oldest and most important
Elves in all of Middle-earth lore, and that

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:07.990
granted him a brief cameo appearance in the
Lord of the Rings film trilogy, as a ring-bearer

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:10.640
in the prologue and at the Grey Havens in
the end.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:15.140
That said, there's so much more to his story
that a TV series could explore.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.620
Círdan was born before the First Age, and
by the time he finally left Middle-earth in

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:23.510
the Fourth Age, he'd been the oldest elf on
the continent for millennia.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:27.830
In the Second Age, he established the Grey
Havens in the west of Middle-earth as a master

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:29.920
shipwright who loved to live near the water.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:34.460
When Sauron attempted to deceive the Elves
into crafting the Rings of Power, Círdan

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:38.540
was one of the Elven leaders who resisted
the temptation, and when the Three Elven Rings

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:42.020
were hidden from Sauron, he was tasked with
bearing one of them.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:46.240
He fought in the War of the Last Alliance
right up until its end, and in the books it

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.520
was he, not Elrond, who tried to convince
Isildur to destroy the One Ring.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:52.080
"Cast it into the fire!

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:53.300
Destroy it!"

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:58.260
When we first meet Gandalf in Tolkien's The
Hobbit, he is presented as a wizard, but perhaps

00:04:58.260 --> 00:04:59.140
a human one.

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:04.460
Over time, though, Tolkien laid out a complex
mythology for Gandalf and his wizard brethren,

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:09.090
and revealed them to be Maiar spirits sent
by the Valar 1,000 years into the Third Age

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:10.860
to aid against the rise of Sauron.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:15.800
The Lord of the Rings features Gandalf the
Grey and Saruman the White in major roles,

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.400
while Radagast the Brown plays a slightly
smaller part in The Hobbit.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:23.280
But there were two other wizards who didn't
get roles to play in The Lord of the Rings

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:27.600
or The Hobbit, and whose background and deeds
in Middle-earth remain rather vague.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:33.400
In fact, it's possible that these Blue Wizards
- known as Alatar and Pallando - arrived before

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:37.330
the mission was handed down to the others,
sometime in the Second Age.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:41.790
Hoping for their presence in the series might
be in vain, but there's so much untapped potential

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:45.669
in the idea of other wizards and what they
might have been up to that it seems like an

00:05:45.669 --> 00:05:47.880
opportunity that shouldn't be passed up.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:52.560
As Sauron's second in command during the War
of the Ring, the Witch-king of Angmar is a

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:57.330
major presence in the films, particularly
in The Return of the King, and we get to see

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:00.680
him riding into battle on behalf of his dark
master more than once.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:07.080
"Do you not know death when you see it, old
man?

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.580
This is my hour!"

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.480
We even see his death scene courtesy of Eowyn.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:18.240
What we don't ever get to see, apart from
a few hints, is exactly how the Witch-king

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:19.640
became the Witch-king.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.900
We know from the poem that opens The Lord
of the Rings that Three Rings were given to

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:27.280
Elves, Seven to the Dwarves, and Nine to the
Men.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:31.590
We also know from Tolkien's various writings
on the subject that only the leaders of Men

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:35.110
who bore the Nine were fully corrupted by
Sauron's influence.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:39.360
They became the Nazgul, a.k.a. the Ringwraiths,
and the Witch-king was their leader.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:43.940
What we know considerably less about is who
the Witch-king was before he was corrupted

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:48.669
by Sauron, and why exactly he was the one
among the Nine who rose to that particular

00:06:48.669 --> 00:06:49.760
position of honor.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:54.380
It's a mystery the series could shed some
light on in a really interesting way.

